WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:06.714
A leader copies every change to two followers through a log.

2
00:00:06.714 --> 00:00:08.521
Payment 42 needs a new fee.

3
00:00:08.521 --> 00:00:10.528
What should the log entry say?

4
00:00:10.700 --> 00:00:18.408
The simplest entry is the statement itself: update payment 42, and set its fee to one plus a random number.

5
00:00:18.633 --> 00:00:20.577
The leader runs it.

6
00:00:20.577 --> 00:00:25.281
Its random number is 0.37, so the fee is 1.37.

7
00:00:25.500 --> 00:00:31.800
Each follower receives the same statement, and runs it again, later, with its own random generator.

8
00:00:32.000 --> 00:00:34.144
Follower one gets 1.82.

9
00:00:34.144 --> 00:00:36.287
Follower two gets 1.05.

10
00:00:36.287 --> 00:00:40.575
Three copies of the same payment now disagree.

11
00:00:40.733 --> 00:00:45.083
The function now, which reads each server's own clock, breaks the same way.

12
00:00:45.083 --> 00:00:49.664
Anything that depends on where or when a statement runs can come out different.

13
00:00:49.867 --> 00:00:59.345
PostgreSQL ships something else: its write-ahead log, the record it already keeps of which bytes changed on which 8 kilobyte page of the table's file.

14
00:00:59.533 --> 00:01:01.844
There is nothing left to compute.

15
00:01:01.844 --> 00:01:07.376
Each follower writes the same bytes at the same place, and all three show 1.37.

16
00:01:07.567 --> 00:01:11.562
But those bytes are in one major version's private format.

17
00:01:11.562 --> 00:01:17.692
Upgrade follower two to version 18 first, and it can't replay the records that 17 writes.

18
00:01:17.900 --> 00:01:21.462
So you can't upgrade a follower first and fail over to it.

19
00:01:21.462 --> 00:01:24.655
Every server upgrades together, with a short outage.

20
00:01:24.833 --> 00:01:27.791
A logical log sits between the two.

21
00:01:27.791 --> 00:01:34.383
Each entry names one row and the values it now holds: row 42 now has fee 1.37.

22
00:01:34.567 --> 00:01:36.830
The leader computed the value once.

23
00:01:36.830 --> 00:01:41.940
A follower on any version, even another kind of database, only has to store it.

24
00:01:42.133 --> 00:01:47.409
If a reconnect sends the same entry twice, the row still says 1.37.

25
00:01:47.600 --> 00:01:50.973
Log the result the leader computed, written as rows.

26
00:01:50.973 --> 00:01:54.931
Every follower stores the same fee, whatever version it runs.
